          "text": "Freeze and reconcile the support contract\n\nSeveral current claims contradict one another.\n\nReact\n\nThe package README says “React: not required”, but:\n\nreact ^19.0.0 is a mandatory peer dependency;\nthe shipped manager directly imports react;\nthe only declared supported frameworks are React-Vite and Next.js-Vite.\n\nFor 0.1.0, the straightforward honest contract is:\n\nReact 19 is required by the currently supported React-based Storybook frameworks.\n\nLater, when Vue support exists, you can revisit whether React should be supplied through Storybook’s manager runtime, bundled, or represented as an optional peer. But the stable README must agree with the actual package manifest.\n\nStorybook\n\nstorybook ^10.5.0 promises compatibility with future Storybook 10.x minors, while Storyproof uses:\n\nstorybook/internal/*;\nexperimental_getStatusStore;\nexperimental_getTestProviderStore;\nexperimental_TEST_PROVIDER;\nthe undocumented/experimental storyIndexGenerator capability.\n\nThose APIs may be entirely appropriate for an addon, but their stability cannot merely be inferred from the public semver range. Storybook typically ships minors roughly every eight weeks and patches only the latest minor, so this compatibility boundary will be exercised soon.\n\nChoose one of these policies:\n\nConservative first-stable policy: use >=10.5.0 <10.6.0, test exact 10.5.0 plus the newest 10.5.x, and widen after proving the next minor.\nBroad policy: retain ^10.5.0, but add rolling CI against storybook@latest and treat a newly released minor failure as a release blocker.\n\nI prefer the first for 0.1.0. A minor-scoped compatibility line is normal for software built on experimental framework APIs.\n\nPlaywright\n\nThe package currently claims playwright ^1.55.1, but the pending prerelease notes explicitly say only 1.55.1 is exercised in CI.\n\nEither test:\n\nexact minimum 1.55.1;\nnewest currently supported Playwright;\n\nor describe other 1.x versions as best-effort. Because Storyproof derives its container image, browser identity, and baseline compatibility from the installed Playwright version, a floor-and-latest compatibility job would be valuable.\n\nNode\n\n>=22.12 also admits untested odd and future Node majors. The repository has already encountered a Node 24 patch-level Firefox/Playwright transport regression.\n\nFor stable, define the tested line explicitly—something like Node 22 LTS and a known-good Node 24 patch—and document any known-bad Node/Firefox combination until it has been revalidated. Do not let the engines field imply that arbitrary future Node majors are supported.\n\nPublic wording\n\nThe package and root descriptions still say Storyproof captures Chromium screenshots, even though Firefox and WebKit are now supported. The storage example also uses chromium-1280x720@1x, while the actual documented environment identity is platform-prefixed, such as linux-chromium-1280x720@1x.\n\nI would do a deliberate terminology sweep before stable:\n\n“browser screenshots, Chromium by default”;\n“WebKit,” never “Safari”;\nplatform-prefixed storage examples;\ncontainer versus host environment identities;\ncurrent schema and migration behavior.\n\nThese are contract bugs, not cosmetic wording.",
