          "text": "The reviewer dropped the following:\n\n```\n## Review verdict\n\n**Request changes before merging as the stable-release hardening PR.**\n\nI reviewed the current head, `9a7fac4`. The PR is still a mergeable draft. Its CI, workflow lint, CodeQL, and zizmor checks are green; the acceptance matrix includes 24 successful jobs, including the new Playwright-floor and Storybook-latest probes.\n\nPer your clarification, I am **not** counting the missing website or PNG icon implementation as defects in PR #45. The metadata may point to the future PNG as long as that separate deployment lands before the stable package is published.\n\n## Blocking findings\n\n### 1. [P1] The new `playwright@^1.38.0` range is incompatible with Storyproof’s container mode\n\nThe PR declares all Playwright versions from 1.38 through the rest of 1.x as supported, but the floor job tests only **host Chromium on Ubuntu 22.04**. It does not exercise container capture, Firefox, or WebKit at the floor.\n\nThis is not merely missing coverage. A valid `playwright@1.38.0` consumer will break in `capture: { container: true }` for two independent reasons:\n\n1. Storyproof always derives `mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v<version>-noble`. Playwright 1.38’s source contains only Focal and Jammy Docker definitions; Noble was not present. Noble support exists by Playwright 1.45.\n2. Storyproof always invokes `playwright run-server --host 0.0.0.0`. The 1.38 CLI has no `--host` option; that option exists in 1.45.\n\nThe cleanest stable contract is:\n\n```json\n\"peerDependencies\": {\n  \"playwright\": \">=1.45.0 <2\",\n  \"storybook\": \"^10.5.0\"\n}\n```\n\nThen make the floor probe exercise at least:\n\n```yaml\nnode: 22\nbrowser: chromium\ntopology: container\nplaywright: \"1.45.0\"\nos: ubuntu-24.04\n```\n\nPreferably, run the complete three-engine × two-topology matrix at the minimum Playwright version. If host support from 1.38 is important, the alternative is to make container image selection and `run-server` arguments version-aware, and document that container capture has a later minimum than host capture.\n\n### 2. [P1] The supposedly blocking Playwright-floor job is not actually part of the protected merge gate\n\nThe PR describes the `playwright@1.38.0` entry as blocking. However, the active ruleset requires the twelve ordinary acceptance contexts and does **not** require:\n\n```text\nacceptance - node 22 - chromium - host - pw 1.38.0\n```\n\nTherefore, the repository’s enforced required-status-check gate does not include this floor test.  GitHub only enforces the status contexts selected as required by the applicable ruleset. ([GitHub Docs][1])\n\nAfter deciding the corrected floor, either add its exact context to the ruleset or, preferably, introduce one stable aggregate job such as:\n\n```yaml\nrequired-ci:\n  name: required CI\n  if: always()\n  needs:\n    - lint\n    - typecheck\n    - format\n    - test\n    - package\n    - acceptance\n    - example-baselines\n    - tarball-install\n  runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n  steps:\n    - name: Require every blocking job\n      env:\n        NEEDS: ${{ toJSON(needs) }}\n      run: |\n        node -e '\n          const needs = JSON.parse(process.env.NEEDS);\n          const failed = Object.entries(needs)\n            .filter(([, job]) => job.result !== \"success\")\n            .map(([name, job]) => `${name}: ${job.result}`);\n          if (failed.length) {\n            console.error(failed.join(\"\\n\"));\n            process.exit(1);\n          }\n        '\n```\n\nThen protect only the stable `required CI` context. That avoids manually synchronizing the ruleset every time a matrix entry is added or renamed.\n\n### 3. [P1] The Node support contract now contradicts the canonical examples\n\nThe PR lowers the root, package, and both examples from `>=22.12` to `>=22`, while the canonical examples use Vite `^8.1.5`. Vite 8.1.5 itself requires Node `^20.19.0 || >=22.12.0`. As written, the examples claim support for Node 22.0–22.11 while their dependency graph rejects those versions.\n\nThe CI entries `node: [22, 24]` resolve to contemporary patch versions of those majors; they do not test Node 22.0. So the exact newly declared floor is unverified.\n\nI would revert the public and example floor to `>=22.12` and add an exact `22.12.0` boundary test. If Storyproof itself deliberately wants to support Node 22.0 with an older compatible Vite, keep the package engine separate from the examples and prove it using a clean consumer pinned to such a Vite version. The current blanket lowering across the monorepo is not defensible from the existing tests.\n\nThere is also a broader policy choice: `>=22` includes Node 23 and all future majors even though CI verifies only 22 and 24. For a stable package, explicit supported LTS lines would be more honest than an unbounded range.\n\n### 4. [P1] The pending Changeset will generate incorrect release notes\n\nThe existing pending Changeset still says:\n\n```text\nThe declared peer range is ^1.55.1; 1.55.1 is the version CI exercises.\n```\n\nThe PR changes the actual peer range to `^1.38.0`. That Changeset has not yet been consumed by prerelease mode, so its stale wording will flow into a future changelog unless updated.\n\nPR #45 itself also adds no Changeset for its other public package-contract changes:\n\n* removing the React peer;\n* changing Node support;\n* changing the Playwright floor;\n* shipping `docs/` and `CHANGELOG.md`;\n* changing catalog metadata;\n* adding npm-facing quickstart and troubleshooting documentation.\n\nThe `.changeset` directory still contains the older pending entries, but none corresponding to this hardening PR.\n\nAt minimum:\n\n1. Update `playwright-peer-dependency.md` to the final, actually supported floor.\n2. Add a separate patch Changeset describing the remaining package and documentation contract changes.\n3. Ensure the eventual `0.1.0` changelog reads as the complete stable contract rather than preserving obsolete prerelease assumptions.\n\n## Important documentation and catalog findings\n\n### 5. [P2] The newly shipped capture contract contradicts the implementation\n\nBecause `docs/` is now included in the npm artifact, these are no longer merely repository notes—they become versioned package documentation.\n\nThe capture contract currently says:\n\n* Chromium must share the same network namespace as Storybook;\n* running the browser in a separate container is unsupported;\n* split capture cannot work;\n* upgrading “Playwright or Chromium” invalidates every baseline.\n\nBut Storyproof explicitly implements a managed browser container as a supported exception, and users can select Firefox or WebKit. The package README and configuration guide describe that mode as first-class.\n\nThe opening should distinguish:\n\n> **Host capture:** the selected browser and Storybook server share the host network namespace.\n\n> **Managed container capture:** Storyproof owns the supported split topology and rewrites the loopback origin through the Docker host gateway.\n\n> **Arbitrary split capture:** a separately managed container, VM, remote machine, or reverse proxy remains unsupported.\n\nAlso replace Chromium-specific compatibility wording with “the selected browser build.”\n\nThe configuration guide still refers to a “preview support target” and says supported combinations are “release targets, not verified support.” That wording conflicts with the stable positioning and with the extensive CI matrix this PR is adding.\n\n### 6. [P2] The Storybook catalog category is still not encoded correctly\n\nChanging the first keyword to singular `storybook-addon` is correct, but Storybook’s catalog documentation says the **next keyword must be the category**. The package currently begins:\n\n```json\n[\n  \"storybook-addon\",\n  \"storybook\",\n  \"visual-testing\",\n  ...\n  \"testing\"\n]\n```\n\nThe current catalog category is named `Test` and uses the `test` tag.\n\nUse:\n\n```json\n\"keywords\": [\n  \"storybook-addon\",\n  \"test\",\n  \"storybook\",\n  \"visual-testing\",\n  \"visual-regression-testing\",\n  \"screenshot-testing\",\n  \"snapshot-testing\",\n  \"regression-testing\",\n  \"visual-diff\",\n  \"playwright\",\n  \"chromium\",\n  \"firefox\",\n  \"webkit\"\n]\n```\n\nThe separate website PR can supply the PNG. The metadata path change itself is appropriate.\n\n## Smaller hardening gaps\n\n### 7. [P3] The package inventory test permits docs but does not prove they were packed\n\nThe test title says it verifies `docs/**` and `CHANGELOG.md`, but its positive `arrayContaining` assertions include only the license, README, manifest, and compiled entries. If npm packing unexpectedly omitted all docs, the test would still pass.\n\nAdd:\n\n```ts\nexpect(paths).toEqual(\n  expect.arrayContaining([\n    \"CHANGELOG.md\",\n    \"docs/configuration.md\",\n    \"docs/capture-contract.md\",\n    // existing entries...\n  ]),\n);\n```\n\n### 8. [P3] The PR description no longer describes the PR\n\nThe manually written summary still says React is a mandatory peer and that the floor probe uses Playwright 1.55.1. The final head removes React and lowers Playwright to 1.38.0. The auto-generated section is closer to reality, but the authoritative top section is stale.\n\nRewrite it before marking the PR ready for review.\n\n### 9. [P3] Newly documented npm support is not tested with npm\n\nThe README now gives npm installation commands, but both clean tarball-consumer jobs still install with pnpm. The package-manager-independent parts are well tested, but one small npm tarball smoke would validate peer resolution and package exports under the path now shown to users.\n\nA single React-Vite npm smoke is sufficient; it does not need to duplicate the complete visual matrix.\n\n### 10. [P3] Keep a blocking “highest supported Storybook 10” probe\n\nThe broad `storybook@^10.5.0` peer range follows Storybook’s own addon guidance, and the current `storybook@latest` probe passes. Removing the React peer and externalizing React is also exactly what Storybook’s migration guide recommends.\n\nHowever, `storybook@latest` is intentionally nonblocking and will eventually point to Storybook 11. A durable setup would have:\n\n* a **blocking** highest version satisfying `^10.5.0`;\n* a **nonblocking** `latest` probe for the next major.\n\nThat makes the declared range enforceable without losing forward-looking compatibility information.\n\n## What the PR gets right\n\nThe React change is sound. Storybook explicitly provides React to manager addons and recommends externalizing it rather than listing it as a normal consumer dependency. The packed React-Vite and Next.js-Vite consumers both pass, so this is not merely theoretical.\n\nThe following changes are also good:\n\n* shipping version-matched docs and the changelog;\n* adding a concrete preset quickstart;\n* adding an npm installation path;\n* replacing Chromium-only product wording;\n* correcting the platform-prefixed baseline example;\n* making startup errors engine-generic;\n* adding troubleshooting guidance;\n* changing the catalog icon reference from unsupported SVG to PNG;\n* retaining packed-consumer acceptance;\n* exercising the complete current Node × engine × topology matrix.\n\nThe current head passed host and container tests for Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit on both Node 22 and Node 24. That is strong evidence for the **current locked Playwright version**, but it does not establish the newly declared 1.38 minimum.\n\n## Merge gate\n\nI would approve PR #45 after these are resolved:\n\n1. Make the Playwright range truthful for container capture and test the actual floor.\n2. Add the floor job—or an aggregate CI gate—to the active ruleset.\n3. Repair the Node contract and test an exact minimum.\n4. Correct the pending Changeset and add release notes for this PR.\n5. Fix the shipped capture-contract contradictions.\n6. Put `test` in the catalog category position.\n\nThe website and icon can remain a separate change, with deployment required before publishing the stable npm tag. I reviewed this read-only and did not submit GitHub review comments.\n\n[1]: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/available-rules-for-rulesets?utm_source=chatgpt.com \"Available rules for rulesets - GitHub Docs\"\n\n```",
